{{also|suden|sudeň}}

==German==

===Etymology===
From {{der|de|gml|sūden}} and {{der|de|dum|sūden}}. The form is already attested in {{cog|gmh|sūden}}, which replaces native {{cog|gmh|sunden}} from {{cog|goh|sundan}}. However, the modern form with a preserved monophthong must be directly from Middle Low German or Middle Dutch. The latter's influence is seen in the umlaut (compare the Middle Dutch pronunciation of ''-ū-'' [yː]). All these forms are from {{der|de|gem-pro|*sunþanē}}, an adverb meaning "from the south". The original Proto-Germanic noun {{m|gem-pro|*sunþrą}}, whence {{cog|en|south}} and {{cog|de|Süd}}, has always been restricted chiefly to compounds in continental West Germanic.

